  2. The only way the vault's own staked position is touched is through _delegateCall(LP_LIB, ...). Under DELEGATECALL
  the code runs with address(this) == vault, so every external call it makes (IConvexBooster.deposit,
  IConvexRewardPool.withdrawAndUnwrap, ICurveGauge.withdraw, remove_liquidity*) has the vault as msg.sender/owner.
  Consequently the Convex/gauge staked LP balance and the Curve LP tokens are held at the vault address, never at
  LP_LIB. Whatever access control AbstractSingleSidedLP applies to the redeem/exit path is what gates that delegatecall;
  the attacker never reaches it by calling the lib.
  3. Now take the attacker's actual reachable path: a raw CALL to CurveConvexLib.unstakeAndExitPool(poolClaim, [0,0],
  isSingleSided) at the lib address. Execution context is the lib itself, whose Convex/gauge staked balance is zero and
  whose Curve LP token balance is zero.
    - _unstakeLpTokens(poolClaim) → IConvexRewardPool(CONVEX_REWARD_POOL).withdrawAndUnwrap(poolClaim, false) with
  msg.sender = LP_LIB, whose staked balance is 0. Convex's BaseRewardPool burns from balanceOf[msg.sender] and reverts
  (underflow) for any poolClaim > 0. The gauge branch ICurveGauge(CURVE_GAUGE).withdraw(poolClaim) is identical: Vyper
  subtraction on a zero balance reverts.
    - Even hypothetically past that, _exitPool calls remove_liquidity* on the Curve pool with msg.sender = LP_LIB,
  burning LP tokens from LP_LIB's balance, which is zero → revert. There is no path by which a direct call to the lib
  burns the vault's LP tokens, because token custody follows address(this), and address(this) is the lib on a direct
